🚀 Feature
Create a specialized schema type that can represent None, int, or tuple of ints. Could be named Dims, DimList, ReductionDim, or something else.
Motivation
Currently, the only single schema type that can do this is int[1]?, which is an optional<IntArrayRef> in C++. This type seems to almost work correctly, except that if the function is called from C++ with an integer, the optional<IntArrayRef> is filled with a garbage value. The value must be wrapped with IntArrayRef({<value>}) to get correct behavior:
void func(optional<IntArrayRef> opt_dims) {
  if (opt_dims.has_value()) {
    for (auto dim : opt_dims.value()) {
      std::cout << dim << " " << std::endl;
    }
  }
  std::cout << std::endl;
}

func(23); // This prints out a garbage value
func({23}); // This also prints out a garbage value
func(IntArrayRef({23})); // This correctly prints out "23"

After int[n]? was introduced, I tried to use it to fix #29137, but the issue with optional<IntArrayRef> silently breaks any C++ calls that provide an integer as the dim argument. The C++ API of at::sum cannot easily be changed to prevent callers from providing an int--for one, that would be a BC break, but also, there does not seem to be any way to detect whether the optional<IntArrayRef> argument was initialized in this faulty way.
More details starting here: #43982 (comment)
And an older discussion starting here: #30822 (comment)
Pitch
@zdevito wrote a good pitch here: #30822 (comment)
If we had a dedicated class to represent an optional list of dimensions, we would avoid having to use optional<IntArrayRef>. This dimension list class would have constructors that accept the following:

An integer
A list of integers
A representation of None (perhaps c10::nullopt)

Alternatives
Additional context
I think it's important to consider how this new dimension list class would interact with the Dimname[n] schema type, which accepts None as a valid value. Currently, if a function has one overload where an argument type is Dimname[1], and another overload where that argument is int[1]?, this creates an ambiguous situation since both overloads would accept None. Some additional info: #43982 (comment)
This ambiguous situation compiles without error. Apparently codegen will arbitrarily choose which C++ function gets called (perhaps based on the alphabetical order of the schema overload names). To avoid the ambiguity, we could potentially have something like a DimnameListNotNone schema type that does everything that Dimname[1] does, except that it does not accept None. Perhaps there are other possible solutions though.
@t-vi suggested trying to split Dimname[1] into Dimname?[] and Dimname[], and I haven't tried that yet. Still, it would not be ideal to have to have two separate overloads for the dimname list case.
